<Prose title="How this works" active="method" description="Every charity is a model file that reproduces its evaluator's numbers, re-ranked by values you set, in years of healthy life per $1,000.">
  <h2>Models are files you can read</h2>
  <p>
    Each charity is one file in a public git repository. A model is a list of rows, like a spreadsheet: inputs with a value, and calculated rows with a formula over other rows. Every input says what kind of number it is (<strong>observed</strong> in data, an <strong>estimate</strong> made elsewhere, or a <strong>judgment</strong>), where it comes from and why it has the value it has. The file's history is the model's changelog.
  </p>
  <p>
    Models imported from an evaluator have to reproduce that evaluator's own numbers: every calculated row records the value the original spreadsheet shows, and the build fails if the model drifts from it. Where we disagree with a source, we say so rather than quietly changing its numbers.
  </p>

  <h2 id="healthy-years">One unit: years of healthy life</h2>
  <p>
    Every result is in <strong>years of healthy life per $1,000 donated</strong>. A year of healthy life is one person living one more year in full health; researchers call it a DALY averted. Evaluators count good in different units (GiveWell in years of doubled income, animal analyses in days of pain, the Happier Lives Institute in life satisfaction), so we convert each into healthy years at exchange rates you can change under Your values, beside the rankings. GiveDirectly's cash transfers come to about 4.
  </p>
  <h3 id="animals">Animals</h3>
  <p>
    Every animal model takes the same steps after the evaluator's own result. Its days of pain, with milder pain counted as a fraction of a day, are split by species. Each species is counted in chickens, by Rethink Priorities' estimates of how much it can suffer relative to a chicken: a pig about 1.6 chickens, a farmed fish about 0.2, a shrimp about 0.1. That gives <strong>chicken-equivalent pain days</strong>, which become healthy years through two of your values: how a chicken's suffering compares with a person's (33% by default), and how much healthy life a day of disabling pain costs (about 10 hours). ACE's figures in suffering-adjusted days already carry Ambitious Impact's own species weights; we take those out first, so the evaluator's view still reproduces ACE.
  </p>
  <p>
    Where ACE's models use different numbers for the same change, such as a hen moved out of a cage or a meal replaced, ours use one shared figure, and the row says so: "changed for consistency", with ACE's own number beside it.
  </p>
  <h3 id="climate">Climate</h3>
  <p>
    Climate charities count the tonnes of CO₂ they keep out of the air. We value a tonne by the deaths it would cause: Rethink Priorities estimates about 1.9 deaths per 10,000 tonnes, from extra heat and from the air pollution that comes with burning coal, oil and gas. Each death is valued by the age it comes at, like every death on the site (most heat and air-pollution deaths are of older people), so the "child's life" value under Your values moves climate too. Air-pollution deaths are saved the year the fuel isn't burned; heat deaths come late in the century, so like any future good they are discounted (by default as if 60 years away), and so is the wait until a charity's tonnes are actually avoided. At the defaults a tonne from burning less fossil fuel is worth about a day and a quarter of healthy life, and a charity that cuts one for $1 does about 3.6 years of healthy life per $1,000. Tonnes that come without fuel smoke, such as forests kept standing, count only for their discounted heat deaths, about an hour of healthy life each. The deaths per tonne is one of your values; damage that isn't death, such as lost harvests, is counted as zero, a judgment that makes the default probably low.
  </p>
  <h3 id="wellbeing">Life satisfaction and depression</h3>
  <p>
    The Happier Lives Institute counts good in points of life satisfaction for a year (WELLBYs). Most of those are psychotherapy results, and the same therapy is modelled elsewhere in healthy years through the disability weights of the Global Burden of Disease. We anchor on the disability weights: by default a WELLBY is worth 0.065 healthy years, the rate at which a year of depression one standard deviation lighter counts the same through either route (0.13 healthy years). That rate is one of your values, stated in healthy years, so the two routes agree whatever a healthy year is worth to you. In the Happier Lives Institute's own view we keep its comparison with cash transfers in Kenya (0.575 healthy years a WELLBY), so its numbers still reproduce. Deaths in these models are valued the site's way, by age, not through life satisfaction, and don't move with the WELLBY rate.
  </p>

  <h2 id="one-way">One way to count the same thing</h2>
  <p>
    Evaluators each convert between outcomes their own way: what a death is worth, how depression becomes lost health, what a dollar of cash does, exchange rates between currencies, how much the future counts. In our view every model uses the site's one version, a shared figure or one of your values, and the row is tagged "changed for consistency" with the evaluator's own number beside it; the evaluator's view keeps their number, so their spreadsheet still reproduces. In particular:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Deaths</strong> are valued by age band (under five, 5–14, 15–49, 50 and over), from GiveWell's moral weights, times your "child's life" value.</li>
    <li><strong>Every future benefit</strong> is discounted at your discount rate, including a wait before it starts.</li>
    <li><strong>An older estimate counts less.</strong> See <a href="#estimate-age">old estimates</a> below.</li>
    <li><strong>A won reform</strong> (a corporate pledge, a law, a regulation) counts for the years under Your values before it would have come anyway (5 by default), with the wait until it takes effect discounted and one shared rate of pledges kept.</li>
    <li><strong>Charities that move money</strong> (fundraisers, evaluators, effective-giving organisations) are worth what their money does where it goes: those destinations' own results, read live, so every slider moves them too, with one shared share of new money and one chance of success for an organisation not yet proven.</li>
    <li><strong>A past average isn't the next dollar.</strong> Where a model's cost-effectiveness is an organisation's past spending over everything it achieved (campaign wins, research, money moved, a service's lives saved), it is multiplied by how much the next dollar does compared with that average, a value under Your values (50% by default, from 18% to 100%). Every model says which kind of estimate it is; estimates that are already about the next grant (GiveWell's, Ambitious Impact's, the Happier Lives Institute's) and costs per item delivered don't take it, so nothing is counted twice.</li>
    <li><strong>Best guesses, not conservative cases.</strong> Where an evaluator gives a conservative figure and a best guess, we use the best guess; a model more than ten times its peers says why on its page.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="judgments">Judgments: yours and fixed ones</h2>
  <p>
    The judgments that move many models are yours to set under Your values: how a healthy year compares with income, a child's life, a WELLBY and a chicken's suffering, the discount rate, how much less an old estimate counts, the years a won reform counts, how much the next dollar does compared with an organisation's past average, the deaths per tonne of CO₂ and when they come. Rates that rest on evidence, such as how a doubling of income is valued, are fixed and listed with sources under <a href="/data">shared data</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    Some fixed figures are judgments too, not measurements: species Rethink Priorities doesn't cover (fish, cattle, insects) counted as stand-ins, how Ambitious Impact's welfare points become days of pain, longtermist causes valued at zero in money-moved models, where shifted money would have gone, the share of broiler pledges kept, damage from CO₂ other than deaths at zero, Founders Pledge's education constants and the default share of staff pay below market. They are fixed rather than sliders because each concerns only a few models or sits inside a range set by evidence, and the site keeps one slider per concept. Each is marked "judgment" wherever a model reads it, with its range, which the uncertainty bars use.
  </p>

  <h2 id="estimate-age">Old estimates count less</h2>
  <p>
    Each charity is ranked by its evaluator's newest estimate, however old. Some are a decade old: the Humane Society of the United States is ranked on Animal Charity Evaluators' 2016 review; HSUS isn't among ACE's current recommended charities. Evaluators re-check what they recommend every year or two (GiveWell revises its top charities' models several times a year; Animal Charity Evaluators and Founders Pledge re-evaluate about every two years), and some recommendations are withdrawn rather than renewed.
  </p>
  <p>
    So under Your values → Timing you set how much less an estimate counts for each year since it was published, compounding like the discount rate: <strong>5% a year by default</strong>, from 0% to 15%. At 5% a 2-year-old estimate counts 91% and a 10-year-old one 61%. Every model page says how old its estimate is and what that does to its figure at your settings. A charity that moves money to others takes it twice over, as it should: each destination's figure is discounted for that destination's own estimate, and the result for its own.
  </p>
  <p>
    The default is a judgment, not a measurement. GiveWell's successive estimates of the same programme, which the site keeps (41 pairs, about a year apart), move a lot, typically two-thirds up or 40% down, but they don't mostly move down, so revisions alone would justify no discount. What they can't show is the estimates nobody renewed. An estimate's age is counted from its publication date to the date the site's data was last refreshed (2 November 2026), not the day you read it, so a figure doesn't change by itself. The evaluator's own view never applies this.
  </p>

  <h2 id="theoretical">Purely theoretical models</h2>
  <p>
    Some Ambitious Impact models estimate an idea before the charity existed. We show one only if the charity runs the modelled programme today and no model of its actual work exists; it is flagged "purely theoretical" on its pages and on the rankings. A charity that runs several programmes is weighted by funding.
  </p>

  <h2>Credit for shared outcomes</h2>
  <p>
    Most outcomes are paid for by more than one funder. By default each model uses its evaluator's counterfactual logic, such as GiveWell's adjustments for money a grant pulls in from governments or pushes elsewhere. Switch to <strong>credit by share of resources</strong> and an outcome is shared out by everything that went into it: every funder's money, in-kind costs, and staff's pay below what they could earn elsewhere. Shares add up to 100%, so nothing is counted twice.
  </p>

  <h2>Ranges</h2>
  <p>
    Inputs carry a low, best and high value, taken from the source wherever it gives one. The dot on each bar uses every best guess. The bar runs from every input at the end that lowers the result to every input at the end that raises it, so it is deliberately wide, not a 90% interval. Rankings use the dot.
  </p>
  <p>
    Each charity's page shows where the uncertainty comes from: which inputs move the result most, and how loosely each is known.
  </p>

  <h2>Caveats</h2>
  <p>
    A warning sign marks a charity when its range is very wide (over 100×), when most of its uncertainty comes from judgment calls, or when the analysis it rests on is more than 18 months old.
  </p>

  <h2 id="imported-models">Imported models</h2>
  <p>
    Most models are evaluators' spreadsheets imported whole: every public model GiveWell links from its grant pages, plus HLI's, ACE's and Founders Pledge's (the <a href="https://github.com/heychristoph/charityevals/tree/main/inventory">inventory</a> lists what we found and why anything was left out). Each sheet is traced from its bottom line, every cell it depends on becomes a row, and each calculated row must reproduce the sheet's value. Shared values such as the value of a life and the discount rate are then tied to your values, and statistics several sheets share live once as shared data.
  </p>

  <h2>What this is not</h2>
  <p>
    Not an evaluator's recommendation, and not endorsed by GiveWell, Animal Charity Evaluators or any charity listed. Cost-effectiveness is one input to a giving decision; room for more funding, organisational strength and track record matter too and aren't modelled here yet.
  </p>
</Prose>
